# Rook's camp talks (story/camp_talk.gd): offered after a Long Rest or at camp once the opening `if` holds, each once a playthrough, the first that
# holds in this order: when Rook is Strained or Doubtful (story/approval.gd), the acts' words, then Warm, Close and Devoted.
# Rook's Thread of Fate beats are in rook_coldwake_thread.dialogue. Dark secrets are the world lane's (Raum's gate): nothing here states one.
# Drafted by Gemini (docs/plans/gemini_companions/), edited; every edit is listed in the README there. Lines are unvoiced until the voice lane reads them.

~ talk_strained
if name:rook_coldwake and approval.rook_coldwake <= strained
-> strained
endif
-> END

~ strained
Narrator: Rook sits out past the edge of the firelight where the frost creeps in, scraping dried mud off the rim of his shield with a flat thumbnail.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I have kept the shift log clean since we started down this road, but the entries are getting hard to square with proper ordinance.
interject name:rook_coldwake: The dead are owed quiet ground, sealed stone, and a civil tongue from those walking above them, not boots kicking through their leases.
interject name:rook_coldwake: When a watchman starts to wonder whether the dead are owed less than they were, he wonders what sort of parish he signed on to guard.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I did not carry this ledger out of the north just to watch the bylaws broken by my own platoon.
* You are right. Tell us how to put the record straight. -> strained_1
* The living come first out here. Dead stone can wait. -> strained_2
* Say nothing and stay by the fire. -> strained_3

~ strained_1
interject name:rook_coldwake: Keep your hands off what is buried, hold the line when the night presses in, and leave the headstones plumb.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Do that much, and I can mark the recent infractions down to bad drainage and poor visibility.
approve rook_coldwake +8: You heard my grievance out and asked how to mend the record
-> END

~ strained_2
interject name:rook_coldwake: Logged and noted, then, though we will see how well that policy holds when the earth gives way under your boots.
approve rook_coldwake -3: You brushed off the parish code and told me the dead could wait
-> END

~ strained_3
Narrator: Rook closes his ledger with a dry snap, sets his shield across his knees, and watches the dark beyond the firelight.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Quiet is something, at least.
-> END

~ talk_doubt
if name:rook_coldwake and approval.rook_coldwake == doubtful
-> doubt
endif
-> END

~ doubt
Narrator: Rook kneels near the edge of the firelight, thumbing a stick of grey chalk and staring into the red coals.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I cannot quite get the measure of our marching orders lately, though I have gone over the docket twice since supper.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Some hours we stand firm as a churchyard wall, and the next we cut corners that ought to be left square and undisturbed.
interject name:rook_coldwake: A watchman likes to know whether his company respects the boundary stones or only minds them when the weather is fair.
* Fair question. How would you have us walk this beat? -> doubt_1
* We are surviving a haunted valley, not passing a masonry inspection. -> doubt_2
* Say nothing and stay by the fire. -> doubt_3

~ doubt_1
Narrator: He rolls the chalk neatly into its cloth wrap and gives a slow, level nod.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Treat a sworn word like carved granite, and leave what is buried to its rest. That is all the regulation I ask.
approve rook_coldwake +5: You took my doubts in earnest and asked how to walk the beat
-> END

~ doubt_2
interject name:rook_coldwake: Sloppy work is how trenches cave in, whether you are digging in northern ice or valley mud.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Still, the kettle is hot, so we will let the matter rest till morning.
approve rook_coldwake -2: You laughed off my concern as a petty inspection
-> END

~ doubt_3
Narrator: Rook lets out a measured breath he had forgotten to take and tucks the chalk back into his coat.
interject name:rook_coldwake: We will see what the next mile shows on the page.
-> END

~ talk_act1
if name:rook_coldwake and flag.gl_crashed and not flag.vessla_slain
-> act1
endif
-> END

~ act1
Narrator: Rook sits on an upturned crate with his shield propped beside his boot, pickin' glass splinters from the seams of his heavy coat.
interject name:rook_coldwake: That locomotive came down with no regard for grade, ballast, or municipal right-of-way.
interject name:rook_coldwake: In Syndramas, if a driver parked a carriage through three boundary fences and a stone culvert, the clerk would fine him down to his brass buttons.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Still, the chassis held together better than it had any right to, and nobody on our roster needed a shovel and six feet of gravel.
Narrator: He glances up through the bare branches at the huge, crooked face scowling down from the moon.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Cannot say I care for the lighting ordinance in this valley, though. Endless night plays havoc with a watchman's shift rotation.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Back home you knew when your relief was due because the frost turned pale blue along the eastern ditch.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Here the sky just glares at you like an alderman checking the ledger for a missing farthing, and the damp gets straight into the joints.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Best check I remembered to breathe before we turn in. There, one good draw of cold air, duly witnessed.
* How are you holding up after that wreck? -> act1_1
* None of us expected to wake up in a place like this. -> act1_2
* Put the ledger away and get some rest. -> act1_3

~ act1_1
interject name:rook_coldwake: Ribs are stiff and my fingers feel a touch soft from the valley mud, which is never a welcome sign for the cold-blooded.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Give me a dry kerbstone to sit on and a docket to clear, though, and I will manage my watch.
approve rook_coldwake +2: You asked how I was holding up after the train went down
-> END

~ act1_2
Narrator: He taps the flat of his knuckles against the calfskin cover of his ledger.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Unscheduled stops are the worst sort, especially when the stationmaster has neglected the drainage for a century or two.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Stick close to the shield if the locals get unruly, and we will find the proper exit lane.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You shared the shock of our stranding by the first fire
-> END

~ act1_3
interject name:rook_coldwake: Don't need the sleep myself, but I will rest the joints and keep an ear on the perimeter.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Mind the turf by your bedroll, the ground dips sharp toward the ditch.
approve rook_coldwake -1: You told me to stow the ledger and rest after the crash
-> END

~ talk_act2
if name:rook_coldwake and flag.act2_done >= 2
-> act2
endif
-> END

~ act2
Narrator: Rook warms a tin of grey wax near the embers, workin' a smear of it into the stiff leather straps of his gauntlet.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I have served on barrow details where half the platoon bolted at the first groan from under the flagstones.
interject name:rook_coldwake: You lot walked into that Crooked House on the hill with the rafters screaming and the floorboards foul with old grief, and you stood your ground.
interject name:rook_coldwake: It is rare enough to find travellers who can stomach bad masonry and worse ghosts without losing their formation.
interject name:rook_coldwake: We have taken our knocks since the train came down, no question, and a few fights went closer to the bone than regulation allows.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Yet every time the dark crowds in, somebody steps up to hold the gap beside my shield.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Makes a man check his own kit twice, just to be sure he is keeping pace with the company.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I used to think a proper watch only worked when everyone wore the same brass and answered the same corporal.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Turns out a Mixed Parish Detail can clear a docket well enough when the nerve holds.
* How are your hands holding up against the damp lately? -> act2_1
* It is easier holding the line with that shield beside us. -> act2_2
* Save your strength for tomorrow's march. -> act2_3

~ act2_1
Narrator: He flexes his grey fingers over the low flame, watching the wax dull on his knuckles.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Still taking the chill poorly, if I am honest with the log, and the softness creeps up the wrist when the mist rolls in.
interject name:rook_coldwake: They will hold a shield strap through this deployment, though. I have no intention of filing an early retirement.
approve rook_coldwake +2: You checked on my hands after long nights in the valley
-> END

~ act2_2
interject name:rook_coldwake: It is good heavy timber, even if the brass knocker has a habit of speaking out of turn.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Obliged for the vote of confidence. I will keep the front square as long as you keep the flank.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You said my shield made it easier to hold the line
-> END

~ act2_3
interject name:rook_coldwake: Sound procedure, that. A watchman who wastes his wind by the brazier has none left for the patrol.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Settle down now, and I will keep the embers banked till first march.
approve rook_coldwake -1: You told me to save my strength for the road ahead
-> END

~ talk_act3
if name:rook_coldwake and flag.act2_done >= 5
-> act3
endif
-> END

~ act3
Narrator: Rook sits close to the fire with his ledger open to the back pages, his thumb resting still on the corner of the paper.
interject name:rook_coldwake: You can feel the grade steepening under our boots now, like the last hundred yards before the cemetery gates.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Whatever has been souring the drainage in this valley knows we are coming with the lime and the iron wire.
interject name:rook_coldwake: If you had asked me on the night of the wreck, I would have bet my badge none of us would see the end of the docket.
interject name:rook_coldwake: My own breath was borrowed long before I boarded that train, and a loan like that does not usually draw good interest.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Walking with this company, though, has been the cleanest shift I ever signed my name to.
interject name:rook_coldwake: No fudged tallies, and nobody left to stand a watch alone.
interject name:rook_coldwake: When the big storm breaks over the Hollow, we will plant our heels in the gravel and take the weight together.
interject name:rook_coldwake: After that, if the parish finally goes quiet, I might even remember to sigh on schedule.
* How are you feeling with the end of it so close? -> act3_1
* Whatever comes next, we finish this shift together. -> act3_2
* Get some quiet while we still have the fire. -> act3_3

~ act3_1
Narrator: He looks down at his shield, where the firelight catches the scarred brass of his badge.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Lighter in the chest than I have any right to be, and steadier on my feet than I was up north.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Let the last inspection come when it likes. The ledger is nearly square.
approve rook_coldwake +2: You asked how I felt with the end of the road near
-> END

~ act3_2
interject name:rook_coldwake: Put that down in block capitals, because I intend to hold you to the contract.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Side by side till the last stone is level, and nobody left under the drift.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You promised we would finish the last shift together
-> END

~ act3_3
Narrator: Rook ties the twine around his ledger, lets out one slow, deliberate breath, and leans his shoulder against the pack.
interject name:rook_coldwake: A little quiet before the whistle blows suits me fine. Mind the turf till morning.
approve rook_coldwake -1: You told me to take some quiet before the final stretch
-> END

~ talk_warm
if name:rook_coldwake and approval.rook_coldwake >= warm and level >= 3
-> warm
endif
-> END

~ warm
Narrator: Rook sits square on a dry log, scraping a flake of mud off his heel with a thumb that does not quite feel the cold.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Good discipline around this camp, I will give you all that. Nobody leaves kit scattered across the walk, and the watch roster runs to the minute.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Makes a change from marching alone, where the only conversation is the shield-pin rattling against my elbow. A watchman gets peculiar habits when he walks solo too long.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Don't put this on the docket, mind, because it isn't strictly regulation.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Every town or ruin we pass through, I grade the stonework out of ten as we go.
interject name:rook_coldwake: If a kerbstone or a low wall is still sitting plumb after fifty winters, I tap my heel twice against the base course as we pass.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Old quarry superstition from up in Syndramas, though I wouldn't admit it to a corporal. Lets the mason's shade know a working man noticed a square job.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Gave that culvert back down the lane a solid eight, even with the weeds in the mortar. Daft business for a grown watchman, I suppose.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Wait a tick, there goes my chest forgetting its work again. One proper breath for the log, and we are square.
* Tell us what else gets a ten. -> warm_1
* Your secret is safe with us. -> warm_2
* So that is why you keep kicking the walls. -> warm_3

~ warm_1
Narrator: Rook lets out a small, chalky puff of air that might be a laugh, thumbing the edge of his soggy ledger.
interject name:rook_coldwake: You want a ten, you look for dry-stone field walls where a sheep can't pry a hoof in. No lime, just gravity and a stubborn eye.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Haven't seen a ten in this valley yet, though the funeral home down in Wickermoor comes close. Proper drainage under those slabs.
approve rook_coldwake +2: You asked me how I grade the stonework
-> END

~ warm_2
Narrator: He gives a neat, square nod and settles his heavy shoulders back against his shield.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Much obliged. If word got round that the night watch was grading the parish kerbs for sport, folk would start asking for free inspections.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Good to travel with a crew that knows what belongs in the shift log and what stays by the fire.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You promised to keep my habit off the docket
-> END

~ warm_3
Narrator: Rook looks down at the toe of his boot, where the leather is scuffed grey from a hundred quiet taps.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Purely a structural tap, friend, never a kick. A kick shifts the pointing, and then I'd have to cite myself for municipal damage.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Still, if you hear two clinks in the dark behind you, don't draw steel. It's only me paying compliments to a decent plinth.
approve rook_coldwake -1: You teased me kindly about tapping the kerbstones
-> END

~ talk_close
if name:rook_coldwake and approval.rook_coldwake >= close and level >= 5
-> close
endif
-> END

~ close
Narrator: The firelight catches the grey chalk dust ground into Rook's knuckles as he holds his palms out toward the embers.
interject name:rook_coldwake: When you walk the frost-line long enough, your ambitions shrink down to fit inside a shift log. Keep the perimeter tight, keep the ledger dry, move along.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Before we all tumbled off the Ghostlight Express, I reckoned that was the whole docket for me. Just walking parish to parish until the joints finally seized.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Had a small notion tucked away at the back of the book, though. A headstone raised for every poor soul left in untended ground.
interject name:rook_coldwake: And when the last page had its rubbing, a six-foot plot of my own at the end of the road. Clean gravel, good drainage, and no brass badge.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Used to think of that plot like a forfeited lease, something a bloke like me had no legal claim to ask for.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Walking with this company has shifted the boundary pegs a touch. You lot treat a cold watchman like he's still on the living roll.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Makes a man hope that when the docket is finally cleared, there might be a square yard of quiet with his own name on it.
* We will see those stones raised together. -> close_1
* What does a proper plot need? -> close_2
* Is a quiet grave really all you want? -> close_3

~ close_1
Narrator: Rook touches two fingers to the rim of his shield, his voice dropping low and level over the crackle of the wood.
interject name:rook_coldwake: That goes down in the ledger in firm ink, friend. A shift goes twice as fast when the whole squad carries a share of the stone.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Mind the turf until we get there, and I will keep the line straight in front of you.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You promised to help me raise the stones
-> END

~ close_2
Narrator: He reaches into his coat, pats the damp calfskin of his ledger through the wool, and rests his hand there.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Doesn't want much, just dry loam on a gentle rise where the sour water won't pool. A plain slab of grey slate at the head.
interject name:rook_coldwake: And the grass left to come up wild in the spring, so the bees know the lease is paid.
approve rook_coldwake +2: You asked what my own plot would need
-> END

~ close_3
Narrator: Rook watches a spark drift up toward the crone's face in the moon, his hands resting flat and still on his knees.
interject name:rook_coldwake: When you have spent enough winters listening to the earth shift after hours, quiet is the grandest luxury on the books.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Though I won't deny, sitting round a warm kettle with decent company runs it a very close second.
approve rook_coldwake -1: You wondered if a quiet grave was enough for me
-> END

~ talk_devoted
if name:rook_coldwake and approval.rook_coldwake >= devoted and level >= 8
-> devoted
endif
-> END

~ devoted
Narrator: Rook sets his soggy calfskin ledger on his knee, brushing ash from the binding.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Been looking at the road ahead of us, up toward the Wytchwood and that great crooked timber at the heart of it.
interject name:rook_coldwake: By rights, any sensible municipal clerk would condemn the whole valley for subsidence and post a quarantine notice at the pass.
interject name:rook_coldwake: You aren't turning back, though, and neither am I. If the docket takes us straight up the Crooked Tree and back down the roots, I'm on the roster.
interject name:rook_coldwake: You know what I carry in this coat. The ledger with the rubbings, the chalk, and the hope of a quiet plot when the work's done.
interject name:rook_coldwake: They are the only things keeping my boots pointed toward morning. Everything else went cold in Syndramas a long while back.
interject name:rook_coldwake: If it comes to a hard corner between this company and my own rest, the ledger can burn and the plot can wait.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I know what a watch is worth when it holds, and I won't fail this one. Settle down now, the watch is set.
* We would do the same for you. -> devoted_1
* Keep your ledger safe, Rook. -> devoted_2
* Thank you for holding the line with us. -> devoted_3

~ devoted_1
Narrator: Rook straps the belt twine round his ledger with a firm, practised tug and looks up at you across the flame.
interject name:rook_coldwake: I know it, and that is why the shield stays up. Never marched with a platoon that kept better order in the dark.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Now get your head down while the fire holds. I have the first watch, and the perimeter is quiet.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You said you would stand by me the same way
inspire name:rook_coldwake: swore to hold the line for the party
inspiration
-> END

~ devoted_2
Narrator: He tucks the ledger back inside his lining and pats his chest twice, remembering to draw a slow, steady breath.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Oh, I intend to bring the paperwork home clean if the ground allows it. A watchman doesn't lose his kit unless the ditch caves in.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Just wanted the priority logged in advance, so there is no confusion when the masonry starts falling.
approve rook_coldwake +3: You told me not to give up what I carry
inspire name:rook_coldwake: put the party ahead of his own rest
inspiration
-> END

~ devoted_3
Narrator: The brass knocker on Rook's shield gives one small, settled clink as he shifts his weight to stand guard.
interject name:rook_coldwake: No need for thanks when a man is only doing the job he signed on for. You lot gave a cold guard a proper post again.
interject name:rook_coldwake: Get some sleep while the coals are red. Nothing unruly gets past this shield tonight.
approve rook_coldwake +2: You thanked me for standing watch over the party
inspire name:rook_coldwake: pledged his shield and his last rest
inspiration
-> END
